<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lhalung Palgyi Dorje</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lhalung Palgyi Dorje was a ninth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> monk who, according to later Buddhist histories, assassinated the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a> with an arrow, traditionally in 842, to end his persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The story, which modern historians treat with caution, became central to Tibetan memory of the fall of the empire and is re-enacted in the Black Hat dance of Tibetan monasteries.</p></div>
